"
\h0#preface|Preface|

This document describes \c{libdeflate}, a C++ library for compressing and
decompressing data in the deflate format. For the details of the library
interface refer to the comments in its public headers. For the format itself
see \l{https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc1951 RFC 1951}.


\h1#intro|Introduction|

The \c{libdeflate} library is a C++ implementation of the deflate compressed
data format as specified by RFC 1951. It provides an incremental compressor
and decompressor together with functions that work on the whole data at once
and \c{iostream} adapters that are built on top of them.

Deflate is the compressed format found inside zlib (RFC 1950) and gzip
(RFC 1952) among others. Each of these wraps the deflate data in its own
header and checksum, and \c{libdeflate} leaves this wrapping to the code that
handles the container. What it handles is the deflate data itself, and it
does so strictly: the decompressor accepts every data set that the format
defines and rejects everything else, including a few that other decompressors
let through (see \l{#conformance Conformance} for details).

We begin with a tutorial introduction that builds a small compression utility
three times, once with each of the library's interfaces. Specifically, we want
to quickly get to the point where we can compress and decompress data of any
size in a fixed amount of memory. The remaining chapters then cover the
details that the tutorial passes over.


\h#intro-setup|Project Setup|

Our utility is called \c{pack}. It compresses its standard input to its
standard output and, with the \c{-d} option, decompresses instead. Let's start
by creating a new executable project for it (here we assume our current
working directory is \c{/tmp}):

\
$ bdep new -l c++ -t exe pack
created new executable project pack in /tmp/pack/

$ cd pack
\

To use \c{libdeflate} in our project, we first edit the
\c{repositories.manifest} file found in the root directory of the project and
add the \c{libdeflate} repository as a prerequisite:

\
role: prerequisite
location: https://github.com/iw4x-x64-stage/libdeflate.git#main
\

Next, we edit the \c{manifest} file (again, found in the root of our project)
and specify the dependency on \c{libdeflate}:

\
depends: libdeflate ^0.1.0
\

Finally, we import the library in \c{pack/buildfile}. We won't be writing
tests in this tutorial, so we also remove \c{testscript} that came with the
project, here and in the \c{pack/} directory:

\
$ cat pack/buildfile

libs =
import libs += libdeflate%lib{deflate}

exe{pack}: {hxx ixx txx cxx}{**} $libs

cxx.poptions =+ \"-I$out_root\" \"-I$src_root\"
\

\N|If these files are new to you, see
\l{https://build2.org/build2-toolchain/doc/build2-toolchain-intro.xhtml#guide-add-remove-deps
Adding and Removing Dependencies} in the \c{build2} toolchain introduction for
the details on each of them.|

With the dependency in place, let's initialize our project in a build
configuration:

\
$ bdep init -C @gcc cc config.cxx=g++
initializing in project /tmp/pack/
created configuration @gcc /tmp/pack-gcc/ 1 target
    default,forwarded,auto-synchronized
fetching git:github.com/iw4x-x64-stage/libdeflate#main (prerequisite
    of dir:/tmp/pack)
...
synchronizing:
  new libdeflate/0.1.0-a.0.20261029230247.24de28e312a2 (required
    by pack)
  new pack/0.1.0-a.0.19700101000000
\

As you can see, \c{bdep} fetched the \c{libdeflate} repository and configured
the library in our build configuration along with \c{pack}. We are now ready
to write some code.


\h#intro-whole|Whole Data|

The simplest way to use \c{libdeflate} is to give it the whole data at once.
Here is the first version of \c{pack}, which works this way:

\
$ cat pack/pack.cxx

#include <print>
#include <string>
#include <iostream>
#include <iterator>
#include <string_view>

#include <libdeflate/compressor.hxx>
#include <libdeflate/decompressor.hxx>

using namespace std;

int
main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
  const bool d (argc > 1 && argv[1] == string_view (\"-d\"));

  const string in (istreambuf_iterator<char> (cin), {});

  try
  {
    print (\"{}\", d ? deflate::decompress (in) : deflate::compress (in));
  }
  catch (const deflate::invalid_data& e)
  {
    println (cerr, \"error: {}\", e.what ());
    return 1;
  }
}
\

The \c{deflate::compress()} function is declared in
\c{<libdeflate/compressor.hxx>} and \c{deflate::decompress()} in
\c{<libdeflate/decompressor.hxx>}. Both take the data as \c{std::string_view}
and return the result as \c{std::string}. Everything in the library is in
the \c{deflate} namespace.

Let's build our program and try it on a list of numbers:

\
$ b
c++ pack/cxx{pack} -> ../pack-gcc/pack/pack/obje{pack}
c++ ../pack-gcc/libdeflate-.../libdeflate/cxx{huffman} -> ...
...
ld ../pack-gcc/libdeflate-.../libdeflate/libs{deflate}
ld ../pack-gcc/pack/pack/exe{pack}
ln ../pack-gcc/pack/pack/exe{pack} -> pack/

$ seq 1 100000 >numbers.txt

$ pack/pack <numbers.txt >numbers.z
$ pack/pack -d <numbers.z | cmp - numbers.txt

$ wc -c numbers.txt numbers.z
588895 numbers.txt
212845 numbers.z
801740 total
\

The decompressed data is the same as the original (\c{cmp} prints nothing)
and the compressed data takes a little over a third of its size. Since we
didn't specify the compression level, \c{compress()} used the default,
\c{compression_level::balanced} (see \l{#levels Compression Levels} for the
other levels).

\N|Note that we gave our data file the \c{.txt} extension. The root
directory of our project is in the header search paths (that
\c{cxx.poptions} line in \c{pack/buildfile}), and a file called \c{numbers}
there would take the place of the standard \c{<numbers>} header that other
standard headers include.|

Let's now see what happens if we try to decompress something that is not
deflate data, such as our list of numbers:

\
$ pack/pack -d <numbers.txt
error: invalid stored block length complement 13066 for length 12810
\

When \c{decompress()} encounters a fault in the data, it throws
\c{deflate::invalid_data} with the description of the fault as the message.
Let's decrypt this one. The first byte of our file, the digit \c{1}, has the
lowest bit set and the next two bits clear, which says that this is the final
block and that it is stored as is. A stored block continues on the next byte
with its length and then the length's complement, two bytes each. In our file
those are a newline and \c{2}, which make 12810, followed by a newline and
\c{3}, which make 13066, and 13066 is not the complement of 12810.

The \c{invalid_data} exception is derived from \c{std::invalid_argument}.
Besides invalid data, \c{decompress()} also throws it if the data ends before
its final block does or if anything follows it. For example:

\
$ head -c 1000 numbers.z | pack/pack -d
error: incomplete deflate data
\

\N|Compression accepts any data and therefore never throws
\c{invalid_data}.|

This version of \c{pack} works but it keeps both the input and the output in
memory, which limits the size of the data it can handle. Let's fix that with
streams.


\h#intro-streams|Streams|

The \c{<libdeflate/stream.hxx>} header provides two stream classes:
\c{deflate::ostream} compresses what we write to it into another output
stream and \c{deflate::istream} decompresses what it reads from another input
stream. Here is the second version of our program:

\
$ cat pack/pack.cxx

#include <print>
#include <iostream>
#include <string_view>

#include <libdeflate/stream.hxx>

using namespace std;

int
main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
  const bool d (argc > 1 && argv[1] == string_view (\"-d\"));

  cin.exceptions (ios::badbit);
  cout.exceptions (ios::badbit | ios::failbit);

  try
  {
    if (d)
    {
      deflate::istream is (cin, true /* end */);
      cout << is.rdbuf ();
    }
    else
    {
      deflate::ostream os (cout);
      os << cin.rdbuf ();
      os.close ();
    }
  }
  catch (const deflate::invalid_data& e)
  {
    println (cerr, \"error: {}\", e.what ());
    return 1;
  }
}
\

Let's start with the compressing side. The \c{deflate::ostream} constructor
takes the stream to write the compressed data to and, optionally, the
compression level. The compressed data is complete only after \c{close()}
writes its final block, so we call it explicitly (see \l{#contracts
Programming Errors} for what happens if we forget). The errors of writing to
\c{cout} come through \c{deflate::ostream} as exceptions, which is why it
requires the other stream to have the \c{badbit} and \c{failbit} exceptions
enabled.

On the decompressing side, the second argument of the \c{deflate::istream}
constructor says whether the compressed data must end the other stream. Here
we pass \c{true} and so anything after the data is an error. If we pass
\c{false} instead, whatever follows the data is left in the other stream for
us to read next. In both cases \c{invalid_data} comes through
\c{deflate::istream} as an exception, the same as from \c{decompress()}.

Let's build and try it:

\
$ b
c++ pack/cxx{pack} -> ../pack-gcc/pack/pack/obje{pack}
ld ../pack-gcc/pack/pack/exe{pack}
ln ../pack-gcc/pack/pack/exe{pack} -> pack/

$ pack/pack <numbers.txt >numbers.z
$ pack/pack -d <numbers.z | cmp - numbers.txt

$ wc -c numbers.z
212845 numbers.z

$ (cat numbers.z; echo junk) | pack/pack -d >/dev/null
error: junk after deflate data
\

So far so good. But let's also try an edge case, empty input:

\
$ pack/pack </dev/null
terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::__ios_failure'
  what():  basic_ios::clear: iostream error
\

While not what we hoped for, the problem is in our program. If the stream
buffer that we insert with \c{operator<<} has no characters, the insertion
sets \c{failbit} on the stream, and \c{deflate::ostream}, like \c{cout},
has the \c{failbit} exception enabled. The same happens on the decompressing
side when the decompressed data is empty. The fix is to only insert if there
is something to insert:

\
$ cat pack/pack.cxx

...

    if (d)
    {
      deflate::istream is (cin, true /* end */);

      if (is.peek () != istream::traits_type::eof ())
        cout << is.rdbuf ();
    }
    else
    {
      deflate::ostream os (cout);

      if (cin.peek () != istream::traits_type::eof ())
        os << cin.rdbuf ();

      os.close ();
    }

...
\

Let's rebuild and try again:

\
$ b
c++ pack/cxx{pack} -> ../pack-gcc/pack/pack/obje{pack}
ld ../pack-gcc/pack/pack/exe{pack}
ln ../pack-gcc/pack/pack/exe{pack} -> pack/

$ pack/pack </dev/null | od -An -tx1
 03 00

$ pack/pack </dev/null | pack/pack -d | wc -c
0
\

As you can see, empty data compresses into two bytes: a final block
compressed with the fixed codes that contains nothing but the end of the
block.

Now our \c{pack} can handle data of any size. Both streams keep a buffer on
each side and the compressor keeps a window of the data it searches for
matches, and that is all the memory they need. Let's compress 100MB of
zeros and see how much memory it takes:

\
$ head -c 100000000 /dev/zero | \\
  /usr/bin/time -f '%M KB' pack/pack | wc -c
5052 KB
97199
\

Notice that the 5MB that our program takes includes the C++ runtime and
\c{libdeflate} itself. With ten times as much data we see about the
same figure.

\N|To read the data, \c{deflate::istream} only takes what the other stream's
buffer already holds and, if the compressed data doesn't end the stream, puts
back what it took and didn't use. The other stream's buffer must therefore be
able to put back the characters of its current get area, which the standard
file and string stream buffers can.|

\N|On Windows the standard streams are opened in the text mode, which
translates line endings and so corrupts binary data. A real \c{pack} would
switch \c{stdin} and \c{stdout} to the binary mode with \c{_setmode()}
first.|


\h#intro-incremental|Incremental Interface|

Streams are convenient when the data comes from a stream and goes to one.
When it comes from somewhere else, for example, a network connection or a
memory-mapped file, we can use the compressor and decompressor directly. Here
is the third version of our program, which does that:

\
$ cat pack/pack.cxx

#include <print>
#include <iostream>
#include <string_view>

#include <libdeflate/compressor.hxx>
#include <libdeflate/decompressor.hxx>

using namespace std;

int
main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
  const bool d (argc > 1 && argv[1] == string_view (\"-d\"));

  cin.exceptions (ios::badbit);
  cout.exceptions (ios::badbit | ios::failbit);

  auto run = [] (auto& c)
  {
    char in[4096];
    char out[4096];

    while (!c.finished ())
    {
      cin.read (in, sizeof (in));

      string_view i (in, static_cast<size_t> (cin.gcount ()));
      const bool end (cin.eof ());

      for (deflate::progress p {0, sizeof (out)};
           !c.finished () &&
           (!i.empty () || p.produced == sizeof (out)); )
      {
        p = c.next (i, out, end);
        i.remove_prefix (p.consumed);

        cout.write (out, static_cast<streamsize> (p.produced));
      }
    }
  };

  try
  {
    if (d)
    {
      deflate::decompressor x;
      run (x);
    }
    else
    {
      deflate::compressor c;
      run (c);
    }
  }
  catch (const deflate::invalid_data& e)
  {
    println (cerr, \"error: {}\", e.what ());
    return 1;
  }
}
\

Let's unpack the \c{run} lambda. The \c{deflate::compressor} and
\c{deflate::decompressor} classes have the same interface, so one lambda
handles both. The work is done by the \c{next()} member function, which takes
the next part of the input, the output buffer, and whether this part is the
last one. It returns a \c{deflate::progress} value with the number of input
bytes it \c{consumed} and the number of output bytes it \c{produced}, which
we use to advance the input and to write the output.

The outer loop reads the input in parts and the inner loop hands each part to
\c{next()}. Notice that the inner loop keeps calling \c{next()} after the
input is used up if the previous call filled the output buffer. A single
match can produce up to 258 bytes of output from a few bits of input, so a
full output buffer means there may be more output to write for the input that
was already consumed.

The \c{end} argument says that the data ends within this part. Our program
learns that when \c{cin} reaches the end of its input. For the decompressor,
\c{end} means that the compressed data must end within this part, and if the
input runs out before it does, \c{next()} throws \c{invalid_data}. For the
compressor, it means that there is no more data to compress, so it can finish
the compressed data once it has consumed this part. Until then the
compressor holds on to the data that later input may still find matches in,
and a call to \c{next()} may consume all of its input and produce nothing.
Once we pass \c{end} as \c{true}, we must keep passing \c{true} in the calls
that follow.

Both classes report the end with \c{finished()}, which for the decompressor
means that it has reached the end of the compressed data and for the
compressor that it has written all of it. Note that the decompressor never
consumes input past the end of the compressed data. So if the deflate data is
embedded in another format, whatever follows it remains in the input for the
caller to process.

Let's build our last version and compare its result with the previous one:

\
$ b
c++ pack/cxx{pack} -> ../pack-gcc/pack/pack/obje{pack}
ld ../pack-gcc/pack/pack/exe{pack}
ln ../pack-gcc/pack/pack/exe{pack} -> pack/

$ pack/pack <numbers.txt | cmp - numbers.z
$ pack/pack -d <numbers.z | cmp - numbers.txt
\

The compressed data is the same as what the streams produced. The compressor
output depends only on the input and the compression level, so it doesn't
matter how we split the input into parts.

Both classes also provide \c{reset()}, which brings an object back to its
initial state so that we can reuse it for other data. Note also that after
\c{next()} throws, the decompressor can only be reset, and that the output
written by the call that throws is not reported.



\h1#levels|Compression Levels|

The compression level controls how much time the compressor spends looking
for matches. It is a value of the \c{deflate::compression_level} enumeration
in the range from 0 to 9, with the following enumerators naming the usual
levels:

\
store     0  Stored blocks only, no search for matches.
fastest   1  Shortest search.
balanced  6  The default.
smallest  9  Longest search.
\

Any other value in the range is a level as well. To compress at a level other
than the default, we pass it to \c{compress()}, to the \c{compressor}
constructor, or to the \c{deflate::ostream} constructor. For example:

\
string c (deflate::compress (in, deflate::compression_level::smallest));

deflate::compressor x (static_cast<deflate::compression_level> (4));

deflate::ostream os (cout, deflate::compression_level::fastest);
\

The compressor writes each block in the form that makes it the smallest,
which is one of stored, compressed with the fixed codes, and compressed with
codes of its own. A block holds up to 16384 literals and matches, so at level
0 our list of numbers ends up in 36 stored blocks.

\N|The format lets a match refer up to 32768 bytes back and leaves the choice
of distances to the compressor. Our compressor uses distances of up to 32505
bytes, which leaves room in its window for the lookahead that the search for
the longest match needs.|


\h1#conformance|Conformance|

The decompressor accepts every data set that RFC 1951 defines and rejects
everything else. The format defines some cases only by omission, and the
decompressor rejects those as well. Specifically, it throws
\c{invalid_data} on the first of the following faults:

\ul|

\li|A block of type 3.|

\li|A stored block whose length doesn't match the complement that follows
it.|

\li|More than 286 literal/length codes.|

\li|A literal/length code, or the code of the code lengths, that is
incomplete or over-subscribed.|

\li|A distance code that is over-subscribed or incomplete, with the
exception of a single code of length one.|

\li|A code length repeat with no previous length or past the number of code
lengths.|

\li|A literal/length code with no code for the end of the block.|

\li|Literal/length symbols 286 and 287 and distance symbols 30 and 31 in the
compressed data. These symbols only take part in the construction of the
fixed codes.|

\li|Length 258 encoded with symbol 284. The format encodes this length with
symbol 285.|

\li|A match in a block without distance codes.|

\li|A match that refers back before the start of the data.|

\li|Data that ends before its final block does.|

|

The decompressor accepts 32 distance codes in a dynamic block header, which
the format allows, with only the first 30 used in the data. It also accepts a
block whose distance code has no codes at all, as long as that block has no
matches.

The compressor only writes data that the decompressor accepts. In particular,
it never uses the reserved symbols, and its only incomplete code is a single
distance code of length one.


\h1#contracts|Programming Errors|

Using the library in a way that its documentation rules out is a programming
error, which the library reports as a contract violation. Recall that in the
streams version of \c{pack} we call \c{close()} on \c{deflate::ostream}
explicitly. Let's see what happens if we forget:

\
$ pack/pack <numbers.txt >/dev/null
stream.cxx:262: error: precondition violated: !is_open () ||
    !good () || uncaught_exceptions () != 0
stream.cxx:262: note: in '~ostream'
\

A \c{deflate::ostream} that is destroyed while still open would leave the
compressed data incomplete, so it treats this as a programming error unless
the stream is no longer good or an exception is being thrown.

By default, a contract violation prints a report like this to \c{stderr} and
aborts the process. To handle it differently, for example, to throw an
exception in a test, we can install our own handler with
\c{deflate::set_contract_handler()}, declared in
\c{<libdeflate/contract.hxx>}. The handler receives a
\c{deflate::contract_violation} that describes the failed check. If the
handler returns, the default report is printed and the process is aborted all
the same.

The amount of checking is selected when building the library with the
\c{LIBDEFLATE_CONTRACT} macro: \c{0} omits the checks, \c{1} (the default)
checks preconditions and assertions, and \c{2} adds postconditions and
invariants. For example, to run the library's tests at the highest level:

\
$ b test config.cxx.poptions=-DLIBDEFLATE_CONTRACT=2
\
"
